Here's a C++ function that takes a reference to a `std::string` containing markdown formatted text and returns a `std::string` containing HTML formatted text:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <regex>
#include <string>

std::string markdownToHtml(const std::string& markdown) {
    std::string html = markdown;
    std::regex re(R"(<[^>]*>");
    std::regex_replace(html, re, "<$1>");
    return html;
}

int main() {
    std::string markdown = "<h1>Hello, World!</h1>";
    std::string html = markdownToHtml(markdown);
    std::cout << "Markdown: " << markdown << std::endl;
    std::cout << "HTML: " << html << std::endl;
    return 0;
}
```

This function uses a regular expression to replace any HTML tags in the input markdown string with the same HTML tags. The regular expression `R"(<[^>]*>"` matches any HTML tags that start with `<` and end with `>`. The `replacement` string `"<$1>` ensures that the matched HTML tags are preserved in the output string.

Note that this function does not handle any complex markdown syntax, as it assumes that the input markdown string only contains HTML tags.

